tel 1.0

base punctuation
import soundness

info   CommonMark Markdown as a typed syntax tree, with HTML, terminal and Markdown-source renderings
detail
    _Punctuation_ parses [CommonMark](https://commonmark.org/) Markdown — the plain-text prose
    format of readmes, comments and documentation — into a typed syntax tree, and renders that
    tree three ways: to HTML through honeycomb, back to Markdown source, and to styled text for
    a terminal through escapade. The parser passes every example of the CommonMark
    specification, both over a whole text and when the same text arrives as a stream one
    character at a time.

    The tree keeps Markdown's two levels apart in the types. **Block** structure — headings,
    paragraphs, lists, block quotes, code blocks, thematic breaks and raw HTML blocks — is the
    `Layout` enumeration, and **inline** structure — text, emphasis, strong emphasis, code
    spans, links, images, line breaks and inline HTML — is the `Prose` enumeration. A
    `Markdown` value records which it holds as its `Topic`: a `Markdown of Layout` is a whole
    document and a `Markdown of Prose` is a run of inline content, and the two cannot be
    confused. Each renders to the kind of HTML which is valid in its place: flow content for a
    document, phrasing content for prose.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val document: Markdown of Layout = Parser.parse(t"# Title\n\nSome **bold** text.")

    document.children.head match
      case Layout.Heading(_, level, Prose.Textual(text)) => (level, text)   // (1, t"Title")
      case _                                             => (0, t"")

    document.html.show   // t"<h1>Title</h1>\n<p>Some <strong>bold</strong> text.</p>\n"
    document.show        // the document as CommonMark source again
    ```

    `Parser.parse` is the way in, from a `Text`, a `Chain[Text]`, an `Iterator[Text]` of
    chunks, or a zephyrine `Stream[Text]`, and because `Markdown of Layout` is `Aggregable by
    Text` a document also reads directly from any source with `read[Markdown of Layout]`.
    Parsing is total: there is no parse error, since every text is some Markdown document,
    and a Markdown document has no notion of a syntax error.

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label A document, parsed
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          val source = t"# Title\n\nA *word* and a [link](https://example.org/).\n\n- one\n- two\n"
          val document = Parser.parse(source)
    frame
      label Its syntax tree, as inspected
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          val source = t"# Title\n\nA *word* and a [link](https://example.org/).\n\n- one\n- two\n"
          val document = Parser.parse(source)

          document.inspect
          // Markdown(linkRefs:[] ╱ children:[Heading(1ˢᵗ ╱ 1 ╱ [Textual(t"Title")]),
          //   Paragraph(3ʳᵈ ╱ [Textual(t"A "), Emphasis([Textual(t"word")]), Textual(t" and a "),
          //   Link(t"https://example.org/" ╱ ○ ╱ [Textual(t"link")]), Textual(t".")]),
          //   BulletList(5ᵗʰ ╱ true ╱ [[Paragraph(5ᵗʰ ╱ [Textual(t"one")])], [Paragraph(6ᵗʰ ╱ [Textual(t"two")])]])])
    frame
      label Rendered to HTML
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely

          val source = t"# Title\n\nA *word* and a [link](https://example.org/).\n\n- one\n- two\n"
          val document = Parser.parse(source)

          document.html.show
          // <h1>Title</h1>
          // <p>A <em>word</em> and a <a href="https://example.org/">link</a>.</p>
          // <ul>
          // <li>one</li>
          // <li>two</li>
          // </ul>
    frame
      label Rendered for a terminal
      code
          import soundness.*
          import strategies.throwUnsafely
          import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

          val source = t"# Title\n\nA *word* and a [link](https://example.org/).\n\n- one\n- two\n"
          val document = Parser.parse(source)

          document.terminal(width = 40)
          // a bold, coloured "Title" over a ━━━ rule; "word" in italics; "link" underlined,
          // coloured and wrapped in an OSC 8 hyperlink; "• one" and "• two"
    ##
    ```

    The library is split into components by what each depends on:

    - `punctuation.core` — `Markdown`, `Layout`, `Prose`, `Parser`, `Formattable` and
      `Translator`, the HTML `Renderable` givens, the `Showable` givens which write Markdown
      source back, the `Aggregable` given which reads a document from a stream, and the
      `formatting` and `construables` choice packages. It depends on honeycomb and is
      platform-neutral.
    - `punctuation.ansi` — the terminal renderer: `Renderer`, `MarkdownPalette`,
      `TeletypeFormattable`, the `terminal` extension, the `markdownPrintable` given and the
      `teletypeFormattables` choice package. It depends on escapade, polysyllabic (for
      hyphenation at wrap points), tessellate (for width-aware wrapping) and harlequin (for
      syntax-highlighted code blocks), so it is JVM-only.
    - `harlequin.md`, part of the harlequin library, contributes the `formattables` package
      to this package: `scalaFormattable` and `javaFormattable` syntax-highlight fenced
      `scala` and `java` code blocks when a document is rendered to HTML.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella. The choice packages — `formatting`,
    `construables`, `teletypeFormattables` and `formattables` — hold givens which are imported
    by name, since `import soundness.*` (like any wildcard import) does not import givens; the
    givens a reader needs without an import, such as `Markdown.Formatting.unbounded` and
    `MarkdownPalette.default`, live in companions and resolve through implicit scope.

    Rendering a document to HTML (`html`) needs no imports; writing it back as source
    (`show`) wraps paragraphs at the width of the `Markdown.Formatting` in scope, which is
    unbounded unless `Markdown.Formatting.bounded(columns)` is given; and rendering it for a
    terminal (`terminal`) hyphenates words at line ends by the `Hyphenation` in scope, which
    hyphenates nothing unless one such as `hyphenations.englishHyphenation` is imported.

refer punctuation.Markdown
refer punctuation.Layout
refer punctuation.Prose
refer punctuation.Parser
refer punctuation.Renderer
refer punctuation.Formattable
refer punctuation.TeletypeFormattable

entry .Markdown   a parsed Markdown document or run of inline content: a list of nodes and the link references
  detail
      A `Markdown` value is a list of `#children`, each a `Markdown.Node`, and the
      `#linkRefs` — the `[label]: destination "title"` definitions collected from the whole
      input — which the parser has already resolved into the links which used them. Its
      `#Topic` type member says what kind of node it holds, and is how the two kinds of
      Markdown are told apart: a `Markdown of Layout` is a whole document, whose children are
      blocks, and a `Markdown of Prose` is a run of inline content.

      Documents come from `Parser.parse`, from `read[Markdown of Layout]` on any streamable
      source (`.aggregable`), or from `.apply` over nodes built by hand; inline runs come from
      `Markdown(prose*)`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      val document: Markdown of Layout = Parser.parse(t"# Title\n\nHello, *world*.\n")
      val prose: Markdown of Prose = Markdown(Prose.Textual(t"Hello, "), Prose.Emphasis(Prose.Textual(t"world")))

      document.html.show   // t"<h1>Title</h1>\n<p>Hello, <em>world</em>.</p>\n"
      prose.html.show      // t"Hello, <em>world</em>"
      document.show        // t"# Title\n\nHello, *world*.\n"
      prose.show           // t"Hello, *world*"
      ```

      Three renderings are available on any `Markdown`. `html` (honeycomb's `Renderable`,
      through `.layout` and `.prose`) gives `Html of Flow` for a document and `Html of
      Phrasing` for prose, so each is well-formed in its place; `show` (`.layoutShowable` and
      `.proseShowable`) writes CommonMark source which re-parses to an equal tree, wrapped at
      the width of the `Markdown.Formatting` in scope; and `inspect` (`.inspectable`) shows
      the tree itself, node by node, with the line each block came from. In
      `punctuation.ansi`, `terminal` renders a document for a terminal.

      Two `Markdown` values are equal when their children are equal: the link references
      take no part, since they have already been resolved into the tree.
  refer punctuation.Layout
  refer punctuation.Prose
  refer punctuation.Parser
  refer punctuation.terminal
  entry .Formatting   how a document is written back as source: the column at which paragraphs wrap
    detail
        The contextual value `show` on a `Markdown` consults. Its one setting is `#width`,
        the column at which paragraph text is word-wrapped, or `Unset` to leave each
        paragraph on a single line. The companion's `.unbounded` given is the default, in
        implicit scope, so `show` works with no import; a bounded formatting,
        `Markdown.Formatting.bounded(columns)`, is bound as a local given where wrapped
        output is wanted. The same default is `formatting.unboundedMarkdownFormatting`, for
        importing by name to override a bounded one in an enclosing scope.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*

        val document = Parser.parse(t"alpha beta gamma delta epsilon zeta eta theta iota kappa\n")

        document.show   // one line
        locally:
          given Markdown.Formatting = Markdown.Formatting.bounded(20)
          document.show   // the same words over several lines, none longer than 20 columns
        ```

        Wrapping changes only the whitespace between words — a softbreak is a space to the
        parser either way — so a wrapped document parses to the same tree as its unwrapped
        source. `Markdown.Formatting` is a zephyrine `Formatting`, so it sits alongside the
        formatting values of the other serializers.
    entry .apply   a formatting with the given width, or `Unset` for no wrapping
    entry .bounded   a formatting which wraps paragraphs at a column: `Markdown.Formatting.bounded(80)`
    entry .unbounded   the default formatting given: paragraphs stay on one line
        In implicit scope through the companion, so `markdown.show` needs no import. The
        same value is `formatting.unboundedMarkdownFormatting`, for importing by name to
        override a bounded formatting in an enclosing scope.
    entry #width   the wrap column, or `Unset` for no wrapping
  entry .Node   the common supertype of `Layout` and `Prose`
      An open trait which the two enumerations extend, and the upper bound of a `Markdown`'s
      `Topic`. A `Markdown` is normally a `Markdown of Layout` or a `Markdown of Prose`; a
      node which is neither is inspected as a quoted `toString`.
  entry .LinkRef   a link-reference definition: `[label]: destination "title"`
    detail
        A reference-style link definition, as the parser collected it from the input. The
        definitions a document declares are kept in `Markdown#linkRefs` after the links which
        referred to them have been resolved — a reference may appear before its definition,
        which is why a streamed document is complete only at the end of its input — and
        `show` writes them back at the end of the document.
    entry #label   the label the reference is looked up by, as written
    entry #title   the link's title, or `Unset` if the definition gave none
    entry #destination   the URL the label stands for
  entry .aggregable   reads a `Markdown of Layout` from any stream of text
      The `Aggregable by Text` given which lets a document be read from a file, a resource or
      any other streamable source with `read[Markdown of Layout]`, and which `construables.markdownConstruable`
      builds on. It is unconditional, since parsing is total, and its streaming path parses
      the chunks as they arrive, retaining only the tree and each open paragraph's text, so a
      long document is never held whole.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dysasymptotics.linearSize

      val readme = t"# Readme\n\nThe first paragraph.\n\nThe second."
      readme.read[Markdown of Layout].children.size   // 3
      ```
  entry .layoutShowable   writes a document back as CommonMark source, wrapped per `Markdown.Formatting`
      The `Showable` given for `Markdown of Layout`. The output is not the text the parser
      consumed — it is regenerated from the tree, with conservative backslash escapes before
      any character which could otherwise open an inline or block construct — but it parses
      back to an equal tree. Paragraph text is wrapped at the `#width` of the
      `Markdown.Formatting` in scope, unbounded by default, and link references are written
      after the blocks. It is in implicit scope through the companion.
  entry .proseShowable   writes a run of inline content back as CommonMark source
      The `Showable` given for `Markdown of Prose`: as `.layoutShowable`, for inline content
      alone, so `Markdown(Prose.Strong(Prose.Textual(t"x"))).show` is `t"**x**"`.
  entry .inspectable   shows a `Markdown` as its tree, node by node, with the line of each block
      The `Inspectable` given, for any `Markdown`. Where `show` gives source again, which says
      nothing about how it was parsed, `inspect` gives the tree in product form, which is what
      a programmer looks at to see what the parser made of an input: each node by its case
      name, with its fields separated by `╱`, an absent `Optional` as `○`, and each block
      with the `Ordinal` line it began on.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Parser.parse(t"# Title\n").inspect
      // t"Markdown(linkRefs:[] ╱ children:[Heading(1ˢᵗ ╱ 1 ╱ [Textual(t\"Title\")])])"
      ```
  entry .prose   renders a `Markdown of Prose` as HTML phrasing content
      The `Renderable` given which gives `prose.html` as an `Html of Phrasing`. Text becomes
      a text node, `Emphasis` an `em`, `Strong` a `strong`, `Code` a `code`, `Link` an `a`
      with its `href` (and `title`, if it has one), `Image` an `img` with its `src`, `alt`
      (the plain text of its content) and `title`, `Linebreak` a `br`, `Softbreak` a newline,
      and `HtmlInline` is passed through as it was written. A link's destination is
      percent-encoded as the CommonMark specification requires.
  entry .layout   renders a `Markdown of Layout` as HTML flow content, with code blocks formatted by the `Formattable`s in scope
      The `Renderable` given which gives `document.html` as an `Html of Flow`. Headings become
      `h1` to `h6`, paragraphs `p`, block quotes `blockquote`, lists `ul` or `ol` (with a
      `start` attribute when the list does not start at 1), thematic breaks `hr`, and HTML
      blocks are passed through. A tight list's items hold their paragraphs' phrasing content
      directly, without `p` elements; a loose list's keep them, as the specification says.

      A code block is offered to every `Formattable` given visible at the call site (summoned
      together as an `Every[Formattable]`), in turn, until one accepts its info string; with
      none, or none that accepts, it becomes `pre` and `code`, with a `language-…` class from
      the info string's first word. `formattables.scalaFormattable` and
      `formattables.javaFormattable` (from `harlequin.md`) are the standard ones, and are
      imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import formattables.scalaFormattable

      Parser.parse(t"```scala\nval x = 1\n```\n").html.show   // highlighted, as spans in a `pre`
      ```
  entry .apply   a document from link references and blocks, or an inline run from prose
      `Markdown(linkRefs, layout*)` builds a `Markdown of Layout` from a list of
      `Markdown.LinkRef`s and any number of `Layout` blocks; `Markdown(prose*)` builds a
      `Markdown of Prose` from inline nodes, with no link references. These are the way to
      construct a tree by hand — for generating Markdown with `show`, or for a transformation
      which rebuilds a parsed document from its `#children`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val document = Markdown(Nil, Layout.Heading(Prim, 1, Prose.Textual(t"Title")))
      document.show   // t"# Title\n"
      ```
  entry .sections   splits a document at its thematic breaks into a list of documents
      An extension on `Markdown of Layout`. The blocks between one `Layout.ThematicBreak` and
      the next become a document of their own, without the break; the result has one document
      per section, each with no link references of its own. A document with no breaks is one
      section.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Parser.parse(t"one\n\n---\n\ntwo\n").sections.size   // 2
      ```
  entry #Topic   the kind of node this holds: `Layout` for a document, `Prose` for inline content
      The type member which `Markdown of Layout` and `Markdown of Prose` refine. It is bounded
      by `Markdown.Node`.
  entry #Domain   a label type member, unrefined by the parser
  entry #linkRefs   the link-reference definitions declared in the document
      The `[label]: destination "title"` definitions the parser collected, already resolved
      into the links which used them. `show` writes them back at the end of the document; a
      `Markdown of Prose` has none.
  entry #children   the nodes, in order: `Layout` blocks for a document, `Prose` for inline content

entry .Layout   the block-level nodes of a document: headings, paragraphs, lists, quotes, code, breaks, HTML
  detail
      The enumeration of CommonMark's block structure. A `Markdown of Layout`'s children are
      `Layout`s, and each case carries the `Ordinal` `#line` of the input it began on, so a
      tool can report the position of a block it objects to. Where a block holds inline
      content, it holds it as `Prose` nodes; where it holds other blocks, as `Layout`s.

      ```tabular
      column  Case
      column  Block
      column  Notes
      row  `BlockQuote(line, layout*)`  a `>` quote  holds blocks
      row  `OrderedList(line, start, tight, delimiter, items*)`  a numbered list  `start` is the first item's number; `tight` is whether the items render without paragraph wrappers (no blank line between them); `delimiter` is the `.` or `)` after each number; each item is a `List[Layout]` of blocks
      row  `BulletList(line, tight, items*)`  a `-`, `*` or `+` list  items as for `OrderedList`
      row  `CodeBlock(line, info, content)`  a fenced or indented code block  `info` is the fence's info string cut at whitespace (`List(t"scala")` for a `scala` fence, `Nil` for an indented block); `content` is the raw code, with a trailing newline
      row  `Paragraph(line, prose*)`  a paragraph of inline content
      row  `Heading(line, level, prose*)`  an ATX or setext heading  `level` is typed as `1 | 2 | 3 | 4 | 5 | 6`
      row  `ThematicBreak(line)`  a `---`, `***` or `___` rule
      row  `HtmlBlock(line, html)`  a block of raw HTML  kept as written
      ```

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Parser.parse(t"1. one\n2. two\n").children.head match
        case Layout.OrderedList(_, start, tight, delimiter, items*) => (start, tight, items.size)   // (1, true, 2)
        case _                                                      => (0, false, 0)
      ```

      A `Layout` renders to HTML flow content as part of a `Markdown of Layout` (through
      `Markdown.layout`); one is inspected, within its document, as its case name and fields.
  refer punctuation.Markdown
  refer punctuation.Prose
  entry #line   the line of the input this block began on, as an `Ordinal`
      `Prim` for the first line. Lines are counted at `\n` alone, and a `\r` before it is
      kept as part of the line. Two trees which differ only in their lines are equal, since
      the `Markdown` which holds them compares children by value.
  entry #children   the inline content of a paragraph, heading or block quote; `Nil` for other blocks
      A `Paragraph` or `Heading` gives its own `Prose`; a `BlockQuote` gives the inline
      content of the blocks it holds, flattened; a list, code block, thematic break or HTML
      block gives `Nil`, since its content is either blocks or not prose at all.

entry .Prose   the inline nodes of Markdown: text, emphasis, code spans, links, images, line breaks, HTML
  detail
      The enumeration of CommonMark's inline structure, which a `Layout.Paragraph` or
      `Layout.Heading` holds and a `Markdown of Prose` lists. Its cases are:

      ```tabular
      column  Case
      column  Inline
      column  Notes
      row  `Textual(text)`  plain text  escapes and entity references already decoded, so `\*` is `*` and `&amp;` is `&`
      row  `Softbreak`  a line ending inside a paragraph  renders as a newline in HTML and as a space in a terminal
      row  `Linebreak`  a hard break: two trailing spaces, or a backslash before the line end  renders as `br`
      row  `Code(code)`  a backtick code span
      row  `Emphasis(prose*)`  `*text*` or `_text_`
      row  `Strong(prose*)`  `**text**` or `__text__`
      row  `Link(destination, title, prose*)`  an inline, reference or automatic link  the reference already resolved to its destination and optional title
      row  `Image(destination, title, prose*)`  `![alt](src "title")`  its content is the alternative text
      row  `HtmlInline(html)`  a raw inline HTML tag  kept as written
      ```

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Parser.parse(t"See [docs](https://example.org \"Docs\").\n").children.head match
        case Layout.Paragraph(_, _, Prose.Link(destination, title, _*), _*) => (destination, title)
        case _                                                              => (t"", Unset)
      // (t"https://example.org", t"Docs")
      ```

      A run of `Prose` renders to HTML phrasing content as a `Markdown of Prose` (through
      `Markdown.prose`), and to source with `show`.
  refer punctuation.Markdown
  refer punctuation.Layout
  entry #children   the content of a link or image; `Nil` for every other node
      Only `Link` and `Image` expose their content here: `Emphasis` and `Strong` hold theirs
      as their varargs field, matched as `Prose.Emphasis(prose*)`.

entry .Parser   the CommonMark parser: `Parser.parse` from text, a chain, an iterator or a stream
  detail
      The entry point to parsing. `Parser.parse` takes a whole `Text`, or input in chunks as
      a `Chain[Text]`, an `Iterator[Text]`, or a zephyrine `Stream[Text] over Credit` (which
      additionally needs a `Buffering`), and returns a `Markdown of Layout`. Parsing is
      total — every text is some document — so nothing is raised, and no `Tactic` is needed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dysasymptotics.linearSize

      val document = Parser.parse(t"# Title\n\nSome **bold** text.")
      Parser.parse(t"# Readme\n\nThe first paragraph.\n\nThe second.".stream).children.size   // 3
      ```

      The chunked forms consume their input incrementally, line by line, retaining only the
      tree and each open paragraph's text, so a long document is parsed without being held
      whole. The result is still a whole tree: a link reference may be defined after the
      link which uses it, so no block is final until the input ends. The whole CommonMark
      conformance suite passes through the streaming entry one character per chunk, so a
      document split anywhere parses as the whole text does. Reading a document straight
      from a source with `read[Markdown of Layout]` (`Markdown.aggregable`) uses the same
      streaming parser.

      Lines are split at `\n` alone; a `\r` before it stays in the line. Tabs count as
      advancing to the next four-column boundary for indentation, as the specification says.
  refer punctuation.Markdown
  refer punctuation.Layout
  entry .parse   parses Markdown to a `Markdown of Layout`, from text or from chunks
      Four overloads: `parse(text: Text)`, `parse(chain: Chain[Text])`,
      `parse(iterator: Iterator[Text])` and `parse(stream: Stream[Text] over Credit)(using
      Buffering)`, the last of which consumes its stream. The text form scans with the
      JVM's vectorised line search and is fastest for text already in memory; the others
      read incrementally. All four produce the same tree for the same input.

entry .Formattable   renders a fenced code block to HTML, for the languages it knows
  detail
      A typeclass for the HTML renderer's code blocks. When a `Markdown of Layout` is
      rendered (`Markdown.layout`), every `Formattable` given visible at the call site is
      collected into an `Every[Formattable]`, and each `Layout.CodeBlock` is offered to them
      in turn: `#format` gets the block's info string (cut at whitespace) and its content,
      and returns the HTML to use or `Unset` to decline, in which case the next is asked, and
      finally the block becomes a plain `pre` and `code`. `Self` is the language's label
      type, so a given reads as `"scala" is Formattable`.

      The standard instances are in the `formattables` package, contributed by
      `harlequin.md`: `scalaFormattable` and `javaFormattable` highlight Scala and Java
      through harlequin's tokenizer, and are imported by name. A `Formattable` for another
      language is a given of this trait in scope where `html` is called.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given ("mermaid" is Formattable):
        def format(language: List[Text], content: Text): Optional[Html of Flow] =
          if language.head != t"mermaid" then Unset else Pre(`class` = t"mermaid")(content)
      ```

      The terminal renderer's counterpart is `TeletypeFormattable`, in `punctuation.ansi`.
  refer punctuation.formattables
  refer punctuation.TeletypeFormattable
  entry #Self   the label type of the language this formattable handles
  entry #format   the HTML for a code block with this info string and content, or `Unset` to decline
      `language` is the info string cut at whitespace, so a block opened with
      ` ```scala title ` gives `List(t"scala", t"title")` and an indented block `Nil`;
      `content` is the code, with its trailing newline. An implementation checks the first
      word and returns `Unset` for a language it does not handle.

entry .Translator   a strategy for translating `Markdown` nodes to HTML, for a custom renderer
  detail
      An interface for a renderer which maps a list of `Markdown` values to HTML flow content
      (`#translate`) and a run of inline content to phrasing content (`#phrasing`). Nothing in
      the library calls it: the standard HTML rendering is the `Renderable` givens
      `Markdown.layout` and `Markdown.prose`, and the trait is a contract a program's own
      translation can implement to be swapped in by its callers.
  entry #translate   HTML flow content for a list of `Markdown` values
  entry #phrasing   HTML phrasing content for a `Markdown of Prose`

entry .formatting   the `Markdown.Formatting` to import by name, for writing Markdown back as source
  detail
      The choice package for `show` on a `Markdown`. Its one member restates the default —
      no wrapping — as a named given, for importing over a bounded formatting an enclosing
      scope has set. A bounded width is not a package member: it takes a parameter, so it is
      bound locally as `given Markdown.Formatting = Markdown.Formatting.bounded(80)`.
  entry .unboundedMarkdownFormatting   paragraphs are written on one line each, however long
      The same value as `Markdown.Formatting.unbounded`, which is in implicit scope already;
      this name exists to be imported where a bounded formatting would otherwise be found.

entry .construables   the media-type mapping which makes `text/markdown` responses a `Markdown of Layout`
  detail
      A choice package for apoplexy's OpenAPI client, whose generated operations decode a
      response body by its media type through the `Construable` givens in scope. Importing
      `markdownConstruable` by name maps `text/markdown` to a document, read through
      `Markdown.aggregable`.
  entry .markdownConstruable   `text/markdown` is construed as a `Markdown of Layout`

entry .Renderer   renders a document as styled terminal text: `Renderer.render(markdown, width)`
  detail
      The terminal renderer in `punctuation.ansi`, which the `terminal` extension and the
      `markdownPrintable` given drive. It turns a `Markdown of Layout` into an escapade
      `Teletype` of a given column width: headings are bold and coloured, with a `━` rule
      under a level-1 heading, a `─` rule under a level-2 heading, and a subdued `###` prefix
      for the rest; paragraphs are wrapped at the width, measuring wide characters as two
      cells and hyphenating long words at line ends through the `Hyphenation` in scope;
      emphasis is italic and strong is bold; a code span is coloured on a background; a link
      is underlined, coloured and wrapped in an OSC 8 hyperlink to its destination, as is an
      image, shown as its alternative text in brackets; a block quote is indented behind a
      `▎` bar; list items hang off a `•` or their number; a thematic break is a `─` rule of
      the full width; and a code block is indented two columns, syntax-highlighted by the
      first `TeletypeFormattable` in scope which accepts its language, or subdued if none
      does. Blocks are separated by a blank line, and a tight list's items are not.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

      val document = Parser.parse(t"# Hello\n\nsupercalifragilisticexpialidocious is a word.\n")
      Renderer.render(document, width = 20).plain   // "Hello" over a rule, the long word broken with a ‐
      ```

      The colours come from the `MarkdownPalette` in scope, whose companion default suits a
      true-colour terminal on a light or dark background; a `Hyphenation` is required, and
      the companion's fallback hyphenates nothing, so `hyphenations.englishHyphenation` is
      imported for English text; and the `TeletypeFormattable`s are whichever are visible at
      the call site, `teletypeFormattables.scalaTeletypeFormattable` and
      `teletypeFormattables.javaTeletypeFormattable` being the standard two.
  refer punctuation.terminal
  refer punctuation.markdownPrintable
  refer punctuation.MarkdownPalette
  refer punctuation.TeletypeFormattable
  entry .render   a document as a `Teletype` of the given width, with the palette, hyphenation and formattables in scope
      `Renderer.render(markdown, width)(using Hyphenation, Every[TeletypeFormattable],
      MarkdownPalette)`. The width is in terminal columns; a block quote's or list item's
      content is wrapped to what remains after its bar or marker. The result is a `Teletype`
      whose `plain` is the text without styling and whose `render(termcap)` is the text with
      the escapes a terminal understands.

entry .terminal   renders a document for a terminal: `markdown.terminal(width = 80)`
  detail
      An extension on `Markdown of Layout` in `punctuation.ansi`, and the usual way to show a
      document in a console: `Renderer.render` with the width defaulting to 80 columns. It
      needs a `Hyphenation` (the companion's fallback hyphenates nothing;
      `hyphenations.englishHyphenation` is imported for English), a `MarkdownPalette`
      (defaulted by its companion) and the `TeletypeFormattable`s in scope for code blocks.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
      import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

      val document = Parser.parse(t"See [home](https://example.org/) here.\n")
      document.terminal(width = 60).render(xtermTrueColorTermcap)   // carries an OSC 8 hyperlink
      document.terminal().plain                                      // t"See home here."
      ```

      Printing a document to the terminal directly, with the width read from the terminal's
      `Termcap`, is `markdownPrintable`'s job.
  refer punctuation.Renderer
  refer punctuation.markdownPrintable

entry .markdownPrintable   prints a document to a terminal at the width its `Termcap` reports
  detail
      The `Printable` given for `Markdown of Layout` in `punctuation.ansi`, which lets a
      document be written to a terminal `Out` directly, rendered by `Renderer` at the width
      of the `Termcap` in scope and converted to ANSI escapes by escapade's `Teletype is
      Printable`. A termcap which does not know its width (the built-in
      `termcapDefinitions` report `Int.MaxValue`) renders at 80 columns, rather than drawing
      rules the width of the integers. It needs the same `Hyphenation`, `MarkdownPalette`
      and `TeletypeFormattable`s as `terminal`.

      It is a top-level given rather than a companion member because its subject,
      `Printable`, and its topic are both owned elsewhere, so it is imported by name:
      `import soundness.markdownPrintable`.

      ```scala fuel
      typecheck
      ##
      import soundness.*
      import soundness.markdownPrintable
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation
      import termcapDefinitions.xtermTrueColorTermcap

      Out.println(Parser.parse(t"# Title\n\nHello, *world*.\n"))
      ```
  refer punctuation.Renderer
  refer punctuation.terminal

entry .MarkdownPalette   the colours the terminal renderer uses, as a case class with a default
  detail
      Seven 24-bit `Chroma` colours, each a field with a default chosen to read on both light
      and dark backgrounds in a true-colour terminal:

      ```tabular
      column  Field
      column  Colours
      column  Default
      row  `#heading`  heading text, and the rules under level-1 and level-2 headings  `Chroma(0x82, 0xaa, 0xff)`
      row  `#link`  link text  `Chroma(0x80, 0xcb, 0xc4)`
      row  `#code`  a code span's foreground  `Chroma(0xff, 0xcb, 0x6b)`
      row  `#codeBg`  a code span's background  `Chroma(0x2a, 0x2a, 0x2a)`
      row  `#quoteBar`  the `▎` beside a block quote  `Chroma(0x82, 0xaa, 0xff)`
      row  `#rule`  a thematic break  `Chroma(0x60, 0x60, 0x60)`
      row  `#subdued`  list markers, heading prefixes, image labels, inline and block HTML, and unhighlighted code blocks  `Chroma(0x80, 0x80, 0x80)`
      ```

      The companion's `.default` given is in implicit scope, so `terminal` needs no palette
      import; another is bound as a local given, usually by copying the default with one field
      changed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

      given MarkdownPalette = MarkdownPalette(heading = Chroma(0xff, 0x80, 0x80))
      Parser.parse(t"# Red\n").terminal(width = 20)
      ```
  refer punctuation.Renderer
  entry .default   the standard palette, in implicit scope
  entry #heading   heading text and the rules beneath level-1 and level-2 headings
  entry #link   link text
  entry #code   a code span's text
  entry #codeBg   a code span's background
  entry #quoteBar   the `▎` bar beside a block quote
  entry #rule   a thematic break
  entry #subdued   list markers, heading prefixes, image labels, raw HTML and unhighlighted code

entry .TeletypeFormattable   renders a fenced code block as styled terminal text, for the languages it knows
  detail
      The terminal counterpart of `Formattable`. When `Renderer` meets a `Layout.CodeBlock`,
      every `TeletypeFormattable` given visible at the call site is collected into an
      `Every[TeletypeFormattable]` and each is offered the block's info string and content
      in turn; `#format` returns the `Teletype` to show, or `Unset` to decline, and a block
      no formattable accepts is shown in the palette's subdued colour. `Self` is the
      language's label type, so a given reads as `"scala" is TeletypeFormattable`.

      The standard instances are `teletypeFormattables.scalaTeletypeFormattable` and
      `teletypeFormattables.javaTeletypeFormattable`, which highlight through harlequin and
      need a `ScalaSyntaxPalette` in scope; they are imported by name.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given ("text" is TeletypeFormattable):
        def format(language: List[Text], content: Text): Optional[Teletype] =
          if language.head != t"text" then Unset else e"$Italic($content)"
      ```
  refer punctuation.teletypeFormattables
  refer punctuation.Formattable
  entry #Self   the label type of the language this formattable handles
  entry #format   the styled text for a code block with this info string and content, or `Unset` to decline
      `language` is the fence's info string cut at whitespace (`Nil` for an indented block)
      and `content` the code with its trailing newline. An implementation checks the first
      word and declines any other language with `Unset`.

entry .teletypeFormattables   syntax highlighting for `scala` and `java` code blocks in terminal output, to import by name
  detail
      The choice package of `TeletypeFormattable` givens for `Renderer` and `terminal`. Each
      claims one language by the first word of a fence's info string, runs the block through
      harlequin's tokenizer, and colours the tokens by the `ScalaSyntaxPalette` given in
      scope — an iridescence `Palette` with a colour for each accent (`scalaKeyword`,
      `scalaString`, `scalaType`, …) — so a palette must be provided alongside the import.
      Without any import, code blocks are shown unhighlighted in the subdued colour.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import teletypeFormattables.{scalaTeletypeFormattable, javaTeletypeFormattable}
      import hyphenations.englishHyphenation

      given ScalaSyntaxPalette = new Palette:
        type Form = Srgb
        def background: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Black
        def foreground: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.White
        def scalaError: Color in Srgb       = WebColors.Red
        def scalaNumber: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gold
        def scalaString: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.LimeGreen
        def scalaTerm: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Silver
        def scalaType: Color in Srgb        = WebColors.Turquoise
        def scalaKeyword: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.Orange
        def scalaSymbol: Color in Srgb      = WebColors.Gray
        def scalaParenthesis: Color in Srgb = WebColors.Gray
        def scalaModifier: Color in Srgb    = WebColors.Orchid
        def scalaComment: Color in Srgb     = WebColors.DimGray
        def subdued: Color in Srgb          = WebColors.DimGray
        def accented: Color in Srgb         = WebColors.White
        def margin: Color in Srgb           = WebColors.DimGray

      Parser.parse(t"```scala\nval x = 1\n```\n").terminal(width = 60)
      ```

      No standard `ScalaSyntaxPalette` given ships with the library, so a palette is always
      the caller's.
  refer punctuation.TeletypeFormattable
  refer punctuation.Renderer
  entry .scalaTeletypeFormattable   highlights a `scala` code block with harlequin, coloured by the `ScalaSyntaxPalette` in scope
      Accepts a block whose info string begins `scala` and declines any other. Needs a
      `ScalaSyntaxPalette` given.
  entry .javaTeletypeFormattable   highlights a `java` code block with harlequin, coloured by the `ScalaSyntaxPalette` in scope
      Accepts a block whose info string begins `java` and declines any other. Needs a
      `ScalaSyntaxPalette` given.

entry .formattables   syntax highlighting for `scala` and `java` code blocks in HTML output, to import by name
  detail
      The choice package of `Formattable` givens for `html` on a `Markdown of Layout`,
      contributed to this package by the harlequin library's `harlequin.md` component. Each
      claims one language by the first word of a fence's info string, tokenizes the block
      with harlequin, and emits a `div` of class `amok` holding a `pre` of one `span.line`
      per line, each token a `code` element whose classes name its accent (`keyword`,
      `string`, `type`, …) and, for a term or type, its role (`binding` or `usage`), so a
      stylesheet decides the colours. Both are `CommonFormattable`s, harlequin's base for
      such HTML formattables. Without the import, a code block renders as a plain `pre` and
      `code` with a `language-scala` class.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import formattables.{scalaFormattable, javaFormattable}

      Parser.parse(t"```scala\nval x = 1\n```\n").html.show
      // <div class="amok"><pre><span class="line"><code class="keyword">val</code> …
      ```
  refer punctuation.Formattable
  entry .scalaFormattable   highlights a `scala` code block as classed `code` elements
      Accepts a block whose info string begins `scala` and declines any other.
  entry .javaFormattable   highlights a `java` code block as classed `code` elements
      Accepts a block whose info string begins `java` and declines any other.

entry .LeafBuilder   a parser-internal builder for a block which holds text: paragraph or code block
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: part of the block parser's working state, which accumulates a
      leaf block's lines and finishes to a `Layout`. Its `finish` resolves the block against
      the link references seen so far. `ParagraphBuilder`, `FencedCodeBlockBuilder` and
      `IndentedCodeBlockBuilder` are its cases.

entry .ContainerBuilder   a parser-internal builder for a block which holds other blocks
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the block parser's working state for the document, a block quote,
      a list and a list item. It holds a buffer of finished `children`, `tryContinue` decides
      whether a line continues the container and strips its prefix, `finish` produces the
      `Layout`, and `closesOnBlank` says whether a blank line ends it.

entry .DocumentBuilder   the parser-internal root container, open until the end of the input
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Continues on every line and finishes to nothing: its children are
      the document.

entry .BlockQuoteBuilder   the parser-internal builder of a `Layout.BlockQuote`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Continues on a line beginning with up to three spaces and `>`,
      stripping the marker and one following space, and finishes to a `Layout.BlockQuote`
      of its children.

entry .ListItemBuilder   the parser-internal builder of one item of a list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Continues on a line indented at least to the item's content
      column (`indent`), or on a blank line; `hadBlank` records a blank line after which an
      empty item closes. Its `finish` is a placeholder: the enclosing list builder collects
      an item's children itself.

entry .BulletListBuilder   the parser-internal builder of a `Layout.BulletList`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Collects `items`, each a list of blocks, for a list of one
      `marker` character (`-`, `*` or `+`); `loose` is set when a blank line separates items
      and `pendingBlank` while one is being decided. Finishes to a `Layout.BulletList`
      which is tight unless loose.

entry .OrderedListBuilder   the parser-internal builder of a `Layout.OrderedList`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. As `BulletListBuilder`, for a numbered list with a `start` number
      and a `.` or `)` `delimiter`. Finishes to a `Layout.OrderedList`.

entry .ParagraphBuilder   the parser-internal builder of a `Layout.Paragraph` or setext heading
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Accumulates lines with `addLine`, stripping leading whitespace;
      `isEmpty` is whether it has none. On `finish` it first extracts any link-reference
      definitions from the start of its text, adding them to the document's references, and
      produces a `Layout.Paragraph` of the remaining text — or nothing, if the paragraph was
      definitions alone. `toHeading(level, refs)` does the same for a setext underline,
      producing a `Layout.Heading`. The paragraph's text is parsed for inline content later,
      once the references are complete.

entry .FencedCodeBlockBuilder   the parser-internal builder of a fenced `Layout.CodeBlock`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Remembers the fence's character, length and indent, and the info
      string cut at whitespace, collects lines with `addLine`, and finishes to a
      `Layout.CodeBlock` whose content is the lines joined with a newline after each.

entry .IndentedCodeBlockBuilder   the parser-internal builder of an indented `Layout.CodeBlock`
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Collects lines with `addLine`, drops trailing blank lines on
      `finish`, and produces a `Layout.CodeBlock` with an empty info string.

entry .InlineData   the parser-internal payload of a node in the inline parser's working list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: a sealed trait of the inline parser's intermediate forms. The
      inline parser tokenizes a paragraph into a doubly-linked list of `InlineNode`s, each
      holding an `InlineData` — text, a code span, inline HTML, a break, a link or image, a
      bracket marker or a delimiter run — then resolves emphasis by the CommonMark
      delimiter algorithm and converts the list to `Prose`. `TextData`, `CodeData`,
      `HtmlInlineData`, `SoftbreakData`, `LinebreakData`, `LinkData`, `ImageData`,
      `EmphasisData`, `StrongData`, `BracketData` and `DelimData` are its cases.

entry .TextData   parser-internal: a run of literal text in the inline list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.Textual`.

entry .CodeData   parser-internal: a code span in the inline list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.Code`.

entry .HtmlInlineData   parser-internal: a raw inline HTML tag in the inline list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.HtmlInline`.

entry .SoftbreakData   parser-internal: a soft line break in the inline list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.Softbreak`.

entry .LinebreakData   parser-internal: a hard line break in the inline list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.Linebreak`.

entry .LinkData   parser-internal: a resolved link, with its destination, title and content nodes
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.Link`.

entry .ImageData   parser-internal: a resolved image, with its destination, title and content nodes
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.Image`.

entry .EmphasisData   parser-internal: emphasis wrapped around a list of inline nodes
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; inserted by the emphasis algorithm in place of a matched pair of
      delimiter runs, and becomes `Prose.Emphasis`.

entry .StrongData   parser-internal: strong emphasis wrapped around a list of inline nodes
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use; becomes `Prose.Strong`.

entry .BracketData   parser-internal: a `[` or `![` marker awaiting its closing bracket
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `isImage` is whether the bracket opened an image; once the closing
      bracket is seen, the marker is replaced by a link or image, left as literal text, or
      skipped if a containing link deactivated it.

entry .DelimData   parser-internal: a run of `*` or `_` delimiters, with its flanking
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Holds the delimiter `char`, the run's remaining `length` (reduced
      as the emphasis algorithm consumes it), and whether the run `canOpen` or `canClose`
      emphasis by the left- and right-flanking rules.

entry .InlineNode   parser-internal: a node of the inline parser's doubly-linked list
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. Holds mutable `data`, `prev` and `next`, which the emphasis
      algorithm rewrites in place.

entry .InlineList   parser-internal: the inline parser's doubly-linked list of nodes
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. `first` and `last` are its ends; `append` adds a node holding some
      `InlineData`, `insertAfter` and `remove` splice the list, and `iterator` walks it
      from the first node.

entry .BulletMarker   parser-internal: a recognised bullet-list marker on a line
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The marker `char`, the column at which the item's content begins
      (`contentIndent`) and the `rest` of the line after the marker.

entry .OrderedMarker   parser-internal: a recognised ordered-list marker on a line
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The `start` number, the `.` or `)` `delimiter`, the column at
      which the item's content begins (`contentIndent`) and the `rest` of the line.

entry .EntityMatch   parser-internal: a decoded HTML entity or numeric character reference
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The `decoded` text and the `end` offset after the reference.

entry .CodeSpanMatch   parser-internal: a recognised backtick code span
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The span's `content`, with the specification's stripping of one
      surrounding space applied, and the `end` offset after the closing backticks.

entry .AutolinkMatch   parser-internal: a recognised `<…>` autolink
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use. The `Prose.Link` for the URL or email address, and the `end`
      offset after the closing `>`.
